A||||6||sqoach||Windows98 problem||||4||
Z||000000||sqoach||05-03-2001||11:45 PM||||My clock seems to be running slow. It seems like it slows down every day. Runs about a minute slow each day or something. Anyone else have this problem? Anyone know what I'm talking about??||||reg||4
Z||000001||Oreif||05-04-2001||12:01 AM||||Your CMOS battery is dying. It's located on the motherboard of the computer. Looks about the size of a quater under a metal clip. (some older ones its a battery case holding a few AAA batteries) You need to pull it out and replace it. This is what maintains the clock and the CMOS memory (BIOS memory) when you power down the computer. Bet if you left the computer on it wouldn't lose time.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cameron||05-04-2001||02:11 AM||||That could be true about the CMOS battery, but if it has always done this it could be that the clock speed jumpers are set wrong...but if it just started happening then yes its the battery...you can get them from almost any hardware store or watch store.  Not many computer stores carry these to tell you the truth, but yes some do<P><P>------------------<BR>Cameron<BR> <A HREF="http://Fiero.NetMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>http://Fiero.NetMARC.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||LarryB||05-04-2001||07:59 AM||||Assuming your PC is network-enabled at least occasionally you can also install an NTP client, Dimension 4 is a decent one that works on W98. <P>Won't reset the date on the BIOS but at least the time/date on the running system will be accurate. ||||reg||6
Z||000004||sqoach||05-04-2001||02:23 PM||||Whoa! I didn't know there was a battery in there. I figured plugging it in was all the power it needed. I'll have to check that out.||||reg||11
Z||000005||theogre||05-05-2001||12:01 AM||||Actually... The clock running slow depends on things....<P>If the machine is on all the time, then the system's Real Time Clock is likely slightly out of adjustment. This is VERY common. Unfortunatly there is little you can do about it. (The clock is sensitive to temperature and to how precise it's crystal is made. System D/T clocks don't use the master buss clock. They run off an independant crystal that is often of poor quality.) The only "fix" for this is to buy an addon clock card with a very precise clock, or to use a software product to sync the clock once every few days. (DO NOT sync you clock any more than required. Time Servers Get really Peaved if people pound them for stuppid B.S. And Yes, They can and will b*tch to you ISP about people who abuse them.)<P>If the system looses time only when it's shut off Then you would suspect the CMOS battery. Unfortunatly this battery is not replacable in the majority of systems. It is either part of a "dallas" type chip or it is soldered to the board. Socketed/Plugin batteries are becoming rather rare. Some motherboards will take a plugin battery if you move a jumper.<P>((Dallas RTC chips have the battery built in. There are a couple companies making "clones" of the Dallas chip. The clones often have shorter life than the real Dallas RTC's. Unfortunatly most of these chips are soldered in.))<P><BR>Addon:<P>If you system is on a network,,,, make sure which clock you are actually pulling time from.<P>Novell clients get the time off the server by default. Servers can have the same time loss causes as desktops. (It the Master Time on a Netware LAN is flaky, ALL servers and clients ont that LAN will also be flaky.)<P>WinNT/2K clients can be told to pull the server time in the login script. This is up to the LAN/WAN admins. Nt servers can have the same problems as Novell in this respect.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Songman||05-05-2001||11:28 PM||||I had this problem with my machine which is on all the time and on a cable modem so I just installed a little program called AtomiClock which sets the clock with NASA at selected intervals... No more problem...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
